      <p>
        In modern cloud-native architectures, webhook ingestion endpoints represent the financial frontline of your application. Payment gateways like <strong>Stripe, Adyen, Braintree, and Paddle</strong> operate under an <strong>at-least-once delivery contract</strong>. If your server takes longer than 2,000 to 5,000 milliseconds to respond with an HTTP <code>200 OK</code>, or if an intermediate load balancer drops a TCP socket, the gateway initiates automated exponential retries.
      </p>
      <p>
        When network jitter causes multiple delivery attempts to reach a horizontally autoscaled cluster of containerized workers simultaneously, catastrophic race conditions emerge. Two workers pull the exact same <code>invoice.payment_succeeded</code> event, concurrently query PostgreSQL, observe no completed transaction record, charge the customer's card or credit user balance twice, and write duplicate records.
      </p>
      <p>
        Solving this challenge requires a battle-tested distributed mutual exclusion architecture. This guide dissects the mechanics of combining fast-path <code>SETNX</code> idempotency checks with the distributed <strong>Redis Redlock algorithm</strong> across multi-region cloud infrastructures.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
        1. The Distributed Race Condition: Why SETNX Fails Under Concurrent Webhook Retries
      </h2>
      <p>
        A naive solution frequently deployed by junior developers relies on a simple single-key check:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 font-mono text-xs text-slate-300 my-4">
        <code>IF redis.exists(event_id) THEN return 200 ELSE redis.set(event_id, "done") ...</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        This pattern suffers from two fatal failure modes:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li>
          <strong>The Check-Then-Act Race Window:</strong> If Retry Request A and Retry Request B arrive within 5 milliseconds of each other at separate API pods, both pods execute <code>redis.exists(event_id)</code> before either pod has written the key. Both receive a negative response and both proceed to execute the duplicate payment transaction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Asynchronous Processing Desynchronization:</strong> Even if using atomic <code>SETNX event_id "PROCESSING"</code>, if worker process A is killed by an out-of-memory (OOM) killer, a Kubernetes node eviction, or an uncaught exception before completing the write, the key remains permanently locked in "PROCESSING" state unless governed by a granular distributed lease with automatic expiration and quorum consensus.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
        2. Redis Redlock Algorithm Mechanics: Quorum Consensus and Clock Drift Safety
      </h2>
      <p>
        To eliminate single points of failure inherent to single-instance Redis deployments (even with asynchronous Sentinel replication, where master failover can drop keys written during the failover window), Salvatore Sanfilippo (antirez) designed the <strong>Redlock algorithm</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Redlock provisions <strong>N independent Redis master instances</strong> (where N is an odd number, typically 5) that do not replicate to one another. The acquisition procedure proceeds as follows:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Timestamp & Random Token:</strong> The worker generates a high-entropy random token (e.g. 128-bit UUIDv4) and captures current local monotonic time.</li>
        <li><strong>Sequential Quorum Requests:</strong> The worker attempts to acquire the lock in all N instances sequentially, using identical key names and random tokens, with an instance timeout (typically 5 to 50ms) much shorter than the overall lock TTL (e.g., 10 seconds) to prevent hanging on partitioned nodes.</li>
        <li><strong>Quorum Assessment:</strong> The worker computes total elapsed time. The lock is considered acquired if and only if: (a) the worker successfully acquired the lock in at least <code>Math.floor(N / 2) + 1</code> instances (3 out of 5), and (b) total elapsed acquisition time is strictly less than lock validity time.</li>
        <li><strong>Clock Drift Compensation:</strong> The actual validity time of the granted lock is defined as: <code>ValidityTime = TTL - ElapsedTime - ClockDriftFactor</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Automatic Rollback:</strong> If the worker fails to achieve majority quorum, it immediately dispatches an atomic unlock script to all N nodes, releasing any partially acquired instances.</li>
      </ol>

