      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
        6. Atomic Lua State Management: Token Verification and PROCESSED State Transitions
      </h2>
      <p>
        In distributed systems, atomicity between unlocking and status setting is vital. Without atomic execution, a subtle edge case can occur:
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose worker A finishes processing after 9.8 seconds on a 10-second lock. Worker A sends a standard <code>redis.set(state_key, "PROCESSED")</code> command, but due to network congestion, the packet arrives after the lock has already expired. Meanwhile, worker B has just acquired the lock. If worker A then issues a non-tokenized <code>redis.del(lock_key)</code>, worker A inadvertently deletes the active lock belonging to worker B!
      </p>
      <p>
        By utilizing atomic Lua scripts evaluated directly in the Redis single-threaded execution queue, the engine checks that the currently stored token strictly matches the caller's UUID before deleting the lock, guaranteeing that expired workers cannot corrupt successor locks.
      </p>

